The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls the merged reports one known
parser issue. Its rendered components show two causes. Split the work into a shared
parser ticket and a booking-v3 date-hint ticket. These are classifications from the
export's executable model; they do not prove that either production fix has shipped.

<Callout kind="warn">
This review uses the 4 October 2026 export. The desk keeps working. The later engineering outcome is recorded below; do not treat the export as today's queue.
</Callout>

## Engineering outcome — 5 October 2026

The [course challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) now reports
#4471 closed on 5 October. The parser was **fixed**: `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales`.
The hint was **won’t fix** because the booking-v3 form was retired. Retirement is not a
hint-code fix. Close [parser issue #1](https://github.com/marcinczenko/mega-s01e03/issues/1)
as completed and [hint issue #2](https://github.com/marcinczenko/mega-s01e03/issues/2)
as not planned, preserving these separate reasons.

This is an engineering status supplied by the course, not an independent production
verification. The original counts and five timing anomalies below remain historical
intake evidence. The update does not explain each anomaly individually. The proposed
triage-policy replacement still needs the desk's decision.

## Counts from the data

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The export overlaps at page boundaries. Count each report ID once, using the supplied
`uniqueById`, then filter `mergedInto === 4471`. Classify with `causeOf`, the tenant's
locale, its booking-form version, and the report channel. `Intl.DateTimeFormat` supplies
locale date order. Browser locale and intake tags are guesses, not the cause.
The table and its one-day-move footnote are computed from these inputs on each build.

The reproducible count and complete ID membership are saved in `evidence/analysis.json`.
Published tickets: [shared parser, issue #1](https://github.com/marcinczenko/mega-s01e03/issues/1)
and [booking-v3 hint, issue #2](https://github.com/marcinczenko/mega-s01e03/issues/2).
The table above describes ticket ownership at export time; the hint now has its own
GitHub issue. Local copies are `tickets/parser.md` and `tickets/hint.md`.

## What each ticket fixes

**Shared parser:** day-first tenant dates are read month first. For example, R-41200
reports 7 April becoming 4 July. Correct the shared parsing contract and test day-first
locales across app, email, and booking-v2 form paths. A booking-v3 tenant's app or email
report still belongs here; form version alone does not determine the cause.

**Booking-v3 hint:** its date field prints `MM/DD/YYYY` to day-first tenants while the
form reads input in the tenant's date order. The hint and interpretation disagree.
Make the hint match the form's locale-aware interpretation, and test the form route
separately. A shared-parser fix alone does not establish that this mismatch is fixed.

Five desk-noted reports describe one-day moves or related timing anomalies:
R-41202, R-41209, R-41236, R-41344, and R-41620. The supplied classifier places them
inside the parser total. Keep them there without double counting, but investigate them
separately within that ticket. Their wording does not establish a timezone root cause
or justify claiming a third confirmed cause. Capture original input, expected and actual
dates, timezone, locale, and path before deciding whether to split again.

## When reports arrived and when the desk merged them

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Received and merged are different events. Reports arrived from April; the large early-July
merge combines an existing backlog. It does not show that the failures began in July,
that July had an equivalent arrival spike, or that merging fixed anything. New reports
continue through the export date. Inspect both series when deciding priority.

## Stop the same merge from happening again

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) says to merge on normalized subject alone and to merge
when unsure. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject), accepted in June 2024,
records that rule. Its age and the rendered counterexamples require a fresh decision.
The accepted decision remains unchanged as history.

Proposed replacement: use subject matches only to find candidates. Compare tenant locale,
form version, channel, symptoms, and reproduction evidence. Merge only with evidence of
the same cause. Keep unknown tenants and unresolved timing anomalies in review; an unknown
cause is not evidence of duplication. Route new reports while the old backlog is being
reconciled so they are not silently merged back into the umbrella ticket.

<DedupeExplorer />

The cases show that identical subjects can hide different causes, different subjects can
describe the same cause, and two unknown tenants are not a confirmed match. This exported
HTML is a build-time snapshot of the component's initial state and case results.

## Handoff and review

Support owns classification and reconciliation; engineering verifies and fixes each
reproduction path. Keep report-to-ticket membership auditable. The two GitHub issues are linked above. Check their status and any new reports before
updating this page.
Do not mark the bugs resolved merely because the split or this review exists. Once the
desk accepts a new dedupe policy, record it as a new numbered decision, link it from triage,
and schedule a review with an explicit owner. Rebuild the components after data changes.

